- op: discuss  # af36
  at: '2026-03-19T00:23:29Z'  # af36
  by: agent  # af36
  actor: jgstern_agent  # af36
  clock: 7  # af36
  nonce: af36  # af36
  message: "Re: prefix tree / hash-based resolver for Flink.\n\nThe current NameResolver uses a dict-based lookup with suffix matching fallback. For killbill (1688 files, 259K lookups), this is fine — 30s total (now 16s with line index fix).\n\nFor Flink (14K files), the bottleneck is likely in suffix matching: when an exact lookup fails, the resolver walks all registered names looking for suffix matches. With ~200K+ registered symbols, each suffix lookup is O(n). If 50% of 2M+ lookups fall through to suffix matching, that's O(n*m) = O(200K * 1M) = catastrophic.\n\nA **trie (prefix tree)** for suffix matching would reduce each lookup from O(n) to O(k) where k is the key length. Estimated improvement: if suffix matching accounts for ~60% of the 100+ minute runtime (60 min), a trie would reduce that to seconds, bringing total from 100+ min to ~40-50 min. Combined with the line index fix (already merged), total could be ~25-30 min.\n\nA **hash-based approach** (pre-computing all suffixes into a dict at registration time) would be even simpler: O(1) lookup. For 200K symbols with avg 3 suffixes each, that's ~600K entries — trivially fits in memory. This would reduce suffix matching to near-zero, bringing Flink total to ~20-25 min.\n\nRecommendation: hash-based suffix index (simplest, biggest win). The implementation would modify NameResolver._register_by_suffix to pre-compute all meaningful suffixes into a dict at registration time."  # af36
